def add(num,title,scans,kind,quote,body,keys,terms,register='',confidence=''):
 NOTES.append(dict(id=f'N{num:02d}',title=title,scans=scans,kind=kind,quote=quote,body=body,keys=keys.split(','),terms=terms.split('|'),register=register,confidence=confidence))

add(1,'The corrected course plan is a programme, not six completed chapters','077–078','unpursued implication','programme préliminaire; et problèmes',r'''The inserted “et problèmes” makes the unresolved status of the general-base Dieudonné functor part of the source itself. These pages **support** Hua’s “Le plan du cours”, but the F-crystal specialization theorem is only a title here: no Newton-polygon statement or proof appears in this package. This **adds** a concrete limit to the README’s Book II/II.2 placement and to any attempt to use the plan as evidence that all six chapters were completed.''','plan','programme|problèmes|spécialisation')
add(2,'Attribution cannot be recovered from the inventory title alone','078, 080–081, 084–091, 107','reading question','Rappels sur la théorie de Dieudonné',r'''The typed sheets and their annotations are visible, but this selection supplies no signature assigning a particular typescript to Delale, Labute, Hakim or Messing. This **agrees with** Hua’s caution about the earlier seminar typescripts and **differs from** taking his internal attribution of the later ribbon typescript as established authorship. The English pencil note on107 remains a separate attribution question; neither the archive title nor a change of language identifies its writer.''','stations,witt,level2','Delale|Hakim|main|auteur|anglais')
add(3,'Coordinate Frobenius and its base of definition','080–081','reading question',r'$(x_i)\mapsto(x_i^p)$; en car. $p$',r'''The coordinate-power map is introduced before the explicit characteristic-p qualifier in the subsequent identities. Hua’s “Les vecteurs de Witt” supplies the characteristic-p restriction and distinguishes it from the integral Witt Frobenius; the frozen text **differs** by retaining the actual order and scope of the wording. A mathematical edition should state precisely which base belongs to each displayed map, while leaving the overwritten shift letter and irregular coordinate tuple as separate reading problems.''','witt','Frobenius|caractéristique|polynomial')
add(4,'From perfect fields to perfect rings; the unfinished Witt continuation','081','unpursued implication','corps [deleted], anneau [inserted]',r'''The replacement of “corps” by “anneau” explicitly enlarges the perfect-point assertion for the comparison involving the direct-limit Witt object. This **supports** Hua’s reading, but the final sentence stops unfinished and the archive foliation jumps before082, so the proposed subsequent group structure cannot be reconstructed from these scans. Hua’s ind-scheme interpretation is useful context, not a licence to replace the source’s “schéma en groupes” or supply the missing continuation. The ensuing DVR and K/W assertions are also written without proof here.''','witt,stations','anneau parfait|ind-schéma|suite manque')
add(5,'The repeated FV relation is source evidence, not two independent relations','082','reading question',r'$FV=p.\mathrm{id}$ written twice',r'''Both displayed products read FV; Hua expressly replaces the second by VF. The source therefore **supports** his report of a repetition while **differing from** the corrected system printed in his exposition. The second dictionary row also reads a doubtful “unipotent” for the operator where Hua has “nilpotent”; these are two local reading/correction issues, not grounds to import the corrected theorem into the transcription.''','dieu','deux fois|nilpotent|unipotent|VF')
add(6,'Nilpotence requires twisted iterates','082, left margin','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified','préciser ce que ça veut dire, en introduisant Frob. et Versch. itérés',r'''This is an explicit instruction to complete the definition used by the circled nilpotence assertions. It **restates** the issue identified in Hua’s Dieudonné subsection: relative Frobenius and Verschiebung have changing source or target under iteration. It is an unfinished explanatory step, not evidence that the author doubts the classification theorem itself.''','dieu','itérés|préciser|nilpotent')
add(7,'Classical Dieudonné theory is invoked rather than proved','082','unproved claim','La théorie de Dieudonné fournit une antiéquivalence',r'''The page invokes the canonical three-part decomposition and states the finite-length module classification, base-change compatibility and the four-entry dictionary without constructing a quasi-inverse or proving the compatibility. It **restates** Hua’s classical-theory section, with the literal anomalies ofN05 retained. The essential local work needed is a consistent variance/twist convention, especially when carrying this dictionary to the six-term display on094.''','dieu,colie','anti-équivalence|changement de base|dictionnaire')
add(8,'The semiabelian example supplies a rank calculation','084','unproved claim','commencer par cas d’un schéma abélien; rang 2a+m',r'''The parenthesis gives a route to proving finite local freeness of the torsion of an extension of an abelian scheme by a torus, but the proof is not written here. The inserted rank formula **supports** Hua’s “Exemples” and anticipates the later definition of BT height on088. A proof must carry both exactness of torsion and representability/local freeness through the extension, rather than infer the rank solely from geometric points.''','examples,extensions','2a|schéma abélien|tores|hauteur')
add(9,'Finiteness of a formal-group kernel is an explicit expectation','084','conjecture','Il devrait être vrai; le noyau devrait être fini localement libre',r'''The expectation concerns G(1) and higher torsion, then more generally the kernel of an epimorphism between formal Lie groups of equal relative dimension, with a proposed converse. Hua’s “Exemples” **restates** this as a conjectural passage and does not verify its generality. The page claims the Artinian case and then assumes the extra condition; that change of logical status must remain visible in any use of the argument.''','examples','devrait|artinien|même dimension')
add(10,'The two formal-group conditions are only “sans doute” equivalent','084–085, page transition','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified','ces deux conditions supplémentaires étant sans doute équivalentes',r'''The qualification attaches across the page break to the two conditions just imposed on the formal group. It **adds** a sharper source locator to Hua’s general list of unfinished assertions: the equivalence is not being used here with a supplied proof. The comparison must retain the base assumptions and distinguish this expectation from the later equivalence with connected BT groups.''','examples','sans doute|équivalentes|formels')
add(11,'Locally constant height is proposed as the missing family hypothesis','084 margin; 086','conjecture','La condition suppl. à rajouter devrait être : fibres de “hauteur” localement constante',r'''The Hasse-zero elliptic-family example accompanies an explicit proposed repair to a fibrewise criterion. It **supports** Hua’s explanation by jumping formal height, while the marginal words make clear that local constancy is a proposed hypothesis here, not a theorem proved on the sheet. Scan086 reuses the same geometric phenomenon for failure of a global connected–étale decomposition, so the two passages should be compared without identifying their distinct statements.''','examples,filter','Hasse|hauteur|localement constante')
add(12,'A limited link to the supersingular-correspondence question','084, 086, 108, 113','own idea','point de Hasse nul; purement infinitésimal. et unipotent',r'''These examples suggest testing the BT behaviour of any proposed correspondence in ANABELIAN_SUCCESSOR A.57 before attempting a global construction. This **adds** a local diagnostic to the contextual suggestion, but neither an elliptic degeneration nor Serre–Tate deformation theory constructs the universal supersingular correspondence requested there. Confidence is medium: the local BT data are explicit, whereas the passage from such data to a correspondence is absent.''','examples,filter,level2,nice12','Hasse|supersing|A.57|Serre','ANABELIAN_SUCCESSOR A.57 [Open, return’s claim; only a truncated statement is supplied]','medium')
add(13,'The connected-BT/formal-Lie equivalence is promised','084–085; 090','unproved claim','prouvera par la suite',r'''The text explicitly postpones the equivalence between connected-fibre BT groups and the indicated formal Lie groups. The formal-group construction on090 **adds** part of the promised mechanism, but refers to “Notes I” rather than supplying its proof in this package. This agrees with Hua’s “Exemples” and “Groupes à Frobenius nilpotent”; a reconstruction must not count a later theorem statement as the missing demonstration.''','examples,frob','prouvera|équivalence|Notes')
add(14,'Étale and toroidal classifications have different variances','085','unproved claim','antiéquivalence; équivalence au lieu d’une antiéquivalence',r'''The correction to “antiéquivalence” and the subsequent dual-local-system construction explicitly distinguish two functors. The page **supports** Hua’s classification by p-adic local systems, but does not prove the equivalences or spell out their descent hypotheses. Keeping the first Cartier-dual system separate from the final Hom-from-μ description prevents a variance error when comparing the classical and crystalline functors.''','examples','antiéquivalence|toroïdaux|représentations|mu')
add(15,'What the toroidal converse actually says','085','reading question','et réciproquement; s’il en est ainsi, car. rés sont p',r'''The insertion constrains the converse to residual characteristic, rather than asserting that every connected BT group is toroidal. It **supports** Hua’s interpretation and rules out a tempting but much stronger reading of the unannotated parenthesis. The scope still needs care for a zero group, so a later formal statement should make its quantifiers explicit rather than promote the marginal shorthand unchanged.''','examples','réciproquement|résiduelles|toroïdal')
add(16,'The earlier field-only restriction is expressly withdrawn','085–086','withdrawn in the same text','qu’on avait eu le tort de ne définir précédemment que sur un corps de base',r'''Scan085 acknowledges that the earlier presentation was too narrow;086 states the filtration over a one-point base and discusses its passage to BT groups. This **supports** Hua’s “La filtration connexe-étale” and supplies both the retraction and its replacement in the present selection. The subsequent failure over a general trait is a separate limitation, not a withdrawal of the one-point assertion.''','filter','tort|point|filtration')
add(17,'The separable-rank criterion needs a finite-level reading','086','reading question',r'rang Sép $G_s=\mathrm{card}\,G(\bar s)$',r'''The displayed condition uses G without a visible level-one index, while the neighbouring conditions concern G(n) and G(1). Hua **changes** this to the separable rank of G(1), explaining why the literal total BT group can have infinitely many geometric points. The source supports the need for a reading/mathematical clarification, but the finite-level correction remains outside the frozen transcription.''','filter','rang séparable|card|G(1)')
add(18,'The EGA reference is questioned and the factorization order is problematic','086–087','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified','Résulte facilement; qui figure dans EGA IV ?; étale suivi d’un morphisme radiciel surjectif',r'''The author questions the reference and leaves the lemma without proof here. The literal order on087 **differs from** Hua’s reversed radicial-then-étale factorization, and the overwritten earlier word confirms that the source order is not a harmless OCR swap. Uniqueness, functoriality and exactness are additional assertions to verify after the order and precise hypotheses have been fixed.''','filter','EGA|factorise|radiciel|exact')
add(19,'Kernel closure and two-out-of-three are cancelled','088; compare 110','withdrawn in the same text','Pour tout épimorphisme de groupes de BT, le noyau est de BT [deleted]',r'''The kernel assertion and the ensuing two-out-of-three sentence are both struck out on088, while extension closure and cokernels of monomorphisms remain with an argument. This **supports** Hua’s “Extensions, conoyaux, hauteur”;110 separately denies a smooth-kernel conclusion from exactness. The two scans document related cautions, but110 is not presented as a dated later correction to088.''','extensions,phi','noyau|épimorphisme|deux|formellement lisse')
add(20,'Why multiplication by p explains the cancellation','088, 110','unpursued implication','le noyau est de BT [deleted]',r'''Hua’s own counterexample, the kernel G(1) of multiplication by p on a nonzero BT group, directly explains why the cancelled universal assertion is too strong. The pages **add** the neighbouring distinction between epimorphy and formal smoothness on110; they do not claim that all failures of smoothness have this single form. This is credit to Hua’s explanatory example, not a new result of these notes.''','extensions,phi','noyau|multiplication|G(1)')
add(21,'The Frobenius criteria depend on absent Notes I','090','unproved claim','Cf. prop.14 des Notes I; Cf. prop.1 des Notes I',r'''Propositions2.1 and2.2 are references rather than reproduced arguments, and the two graded formulations are headings without their conditions. This **restates** Hua’s “Groupes à Frobenius nilpotent” and limits what can be recovered from the page alone. The later Tate-object calculations offer an analogue, but do not supply the Frobenius twists or reconstruct the absent numbered propositions automatically.''','frob,tateg','Notes|Frobenius|gradué',R1)
add(22,'Round G(n) and the Frobenius target exponent must not be normalized silently','090','reading question',r'$G=G(n)$; $G[n-i]^{p^{n-i}}$',r'''The frozen reading uses round parentheses in the premise and exponent p^(n−i) in the target, whereas Hua writes G=G[n] and a p^i twist. This **differs from** his reconstruction in two independent places, beyond the locally uncertain endpoint inequality. His mathematical interpretation is plausible, but it should be tested with a fully typed relative-Frobenius map rather than read back into the manuscript.''','frob','p^{i}|p^{n-i}|Frobenius|G[n]')
add(23,'Finite-order smoothness and the associated formal group are announced','090','unproved claim',r'lisse à l’ordre $p^n-1$; $\bar G=\operatorname{Inf}^{\infty}(G)$',r'''The corollary, formal-Lie/Frobenius-coadic equivalence and theorem2.5 are stated without demonstrations on this sheet. They **support** Hua’s account of the associated formal group, while depending on the missing criteria ofN21. A proof must control the infinitesimal neighbourhoods as schemes and the passage to the inductive limit, not merely identify their points.''','frob','Inf|coadiques|lisse à l')
add(24,'The dimension-duality relation is an instruction to add a result','090, rotated margin','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified',r'donner relation $d+d^{t|*}=h$',r'''The marginal instruction requests a relation not established in the main text. It **restates** Hua’s link to dimension plus dual dimension equalling height, but the exponent on the second d remains visually doubtful in the transcription. It is best catalogued as an unfinished addition, with proof and notation still to supply, rather than as a proved marginal theorem.''','frob,colie,nice4','relation|dimension|hauteur')
add(25,'Existence and finite-level surjectivity of deformations','091','unproved claim','Il existe un groupe de BT G sur S qui prolonge G_o; est surjective',r'''Theorem4.1(a,b) asserts lifting over an affine nilimmersion and surjectivity to the finite-level deformation set. It **restates** Hua’s deformation subsection, but the source does not place the p-nilpotence assumption before(a,b), as his reconstruction does. The two absent proofs and the precise scope of that added hypothesis should be checked against a complete deformation theorem, not inferred from the lecture heading “énoncés”.''','def','Théorème 4.1|surjective|nilpotent')
add(26,'The effective bound and nilpotence convention','091','reading question',r'nilpotente d’ordre $k$; $n\geq kN$; $k=1$',r'''The source gives an explicit bound and then treats k=1 as the special torsor case. Hua interprets “order k” by I^k=0 while also reading k=1 as square-zero, leaving a convention that cannot be made consistent merely by copying his prose. This **adds** a precise question: determine whether the manuscript’s “order k” means I^(k+1)=0, and compare the bound only after that convention is fixed.''','def','kN|ordre|carré nul')
add(27,'The deformation torsor lacks its ideal coefficient','091','reading question',r'$\underline t_{G_o^*}\otimes t_{G_o}$',r'''The written torsor coefficient does not include the nilpotent ideal; a cancelled superscript on the first tangent term remains visible. Hua **supplies** an ideal tensor factor in his formulation, so his statement is an explicit mathematical completion rather than a second reading of the same symbols. A useful comparison must also distinguish a sheaf torsor from the group of global sections over the affine base.''','def','torseur|mathcal{I}|Gamma|t_{G')
add(28,'The direct deformation proof is explicitly unavailable','091','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified','je ne sais pas procéder directement !',r'''The author says that the result is obtained through deformations of truncated BT groups and explicitly lacks a direct method. The sequence092–096 **adds** the planned ingredients to this admission: co-Lie data, deformation Ext groups and finite-level comparison. Hua records the same dependence; the manuscript gives a route and intermediate statements, not a complete proof of the limiting argument.''','def,trunc','directement|tronqués|Illusie',R2)
add(29,'The cotangent-complex definition is admitted','092','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified','Définition admise',r'''The word “admise” is itself a medium-confidence reading, but the sheet is plainly a report using the complex as an input rather than constructing it. This **supports** Hua’s “(A) Généralités” and identifies the first explicit dependency of the deformation programme. The very weak line after “Calcul tronqué” is not evidence for an additional theorem until its words are resolved.''','cot','admet|définition|tronqué')
add(30,'Transitivity, complete intersections and Tor-independent base change','092','unproved claim','Propriétés; Application 2; Application 1; Application 3',r'''The page lists homology vanishing, a transitivity triangle and a Tor condition for base change before its deformation applications, without proofs. Hua **restates** the mathematics but deliberately rearranges the applications into1,2,3; the source order is2,1,3 and includes an explicit arrow linking deformation ambiguity to the earlier morphism problem. The hypotheses attached to each property must survive any exposition of this outline.''','cot','transitivité|Tor|intersection|applications')
add(31,'Ext versus algebra extensions: a local reading problem','092','reading question',r'$\operatorname{Ext|Exal}_{\underline O_S}(\underline O_X,J)$',r'''The first name in the algebra-extension display is not secure enough to identify it with an ordinary module Ext without qualification. Hua’s “(A) Généralités” **interprets** the objects as square-zero algebra extensions and states their classification by the cotangent complex. That interpretation explains the possible Exal reading but does not settle the ink; the source’s displayed arguments and order remain frozen.''','cot','algèbres|extensions|Ext')
add(32,'Co-Lie amplitude and rank need their hypotheses retained','093','unproved claim','G loc. prés. finie; intersection complète relative',r'''The co-Lie and dual Lie complexes are assigned perfect amplitudes, their four homology modules are named, and a rank/dimension relation is asserted. This **restates** Hua’s “(B) Groupes plats” and bears on his co-Lie findings entry, but does not prove these structural facts here. Finite presentation, flatness, grading convention and any local-freeness needed to identify dual homology must remain explicit in a reconstruction.''','colie','amplitude|intersection|dimension|co-Lie',R2)
add(33,'The six-term co-Lie sequence has unresolved indices','093','reading question',r'$n_{G\prime}-n_G\longrightarrow n_G$',r'''The frozen sequence repeats a plain G at the third n-term, whereas Hua restores the usual ordered G″,G,G′ terms from the triangle. The source therefore **differs from** his completed exact sequence, and the plain dashes must also be distinguished from actual arrowheads. This is a reason to type-check the triangle and its homology sequence separately, not to trust the restored sequence as a diplomatic reading.''','colie','suite exacte|surchargés|triangle',R2)
add(34,'The boxed formula and its marginal specialization disagree','093','reading question',r'$R\underline{\mathrm{Hom}}(\ell^G,J)\simeq\tau_{\leq1}R\mathrm{Hom}(G^*,J)$; $t_{G^*}$',r'''The marginal tangent term is written with G* while the boxed formula specializes with a different duality placement, and a nearby “à vérifier !” remains a low-confidence reading. Hua **identifies and corrects** this discrepancy in “(B) Groupes plats”, additionally supplying a finite-local-freeness hypothesis for the Cartier dual. The page records an important formula and an unstable specialization, not two mutually checked identities.''','colie','remarquable|marginale|vérifier|contredit',R2)
add(35,'A perfect two-term object is expected over every base','094','conjecture','devrait exister sur toute base',r'''The final sentence proposes an all-base triangle with a perfect-amplitude [0,1] object read Δ* or Δα. It **supports** Hua’s account of a conditional Dieudonné/co-Lie construction, while the perfect-field display above does not construct that object over a general base. A proof would need a functorial construction, the connecting morphism and compatibility with base change, not just termwise identifications over perfect fields.''','colie,nice4','toute|Delta|conditionnel',R2)
add(36,'The triangle’s connecting arrow is explicitly queried','094','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified','? on the arrow to the shifted co-Lie complex',r'''The question mark belongs to a particular arrow, so it should not be reduced to a general impression that the page is tentative. It **adds** a precise location to Hua’s “(B) Groupes plats”: the degree/sign and the comparison of the two triangles are still under examination. The right-hand triangle’s displayed shift pattern and the blue p insertions in D* also remain reading questions.''','colie','triangle|flèche|degré|conditionnel',R2)
add(37,'A possible bridge from Δ to the later Hodge filtration','094, 115','own idea','Δ; D(G)_S est filtré',r'''Hua already proposes identifying the hypothetical middle object with the later crystalline Hodge construction; these scans **support** that lead but do not themselves state the identification. A useful next step is to compare both triangles with all duals and shifts retained, and test the resulting maps on the three basic order-p group schemes before claiming a functorial equivalence. Confidence is medium because the endpoint invariants match the intended programme, but the uncertain arrow and overwritten Hodge term leave the actual comparison undetermined.''','colie,nice4','Hodge|Delta|ordre',R2,'medium')
add(38,'A coefficient-category correction stops halfway down the sheet','095','reading question',r'$\operatorname{Ext}_{\mathcal O}$ changed to $\operatorname{Ext}_{\mathbf Z}$',r'''The homomorphism-lifting formulas replace the coefficient category by abelian sheaves over Z, while the following group-deformation displays still carry O_(S_o). Hua **normalizes all of them** to abelian-sheaf Ext; the manuscript visibly records only the first correction. This matters because a group scheme need not be an O-module, so the difference is substantive even though the intended repair is clear in context.''','defg','faisceaux abéliens|indexe|mathcal{O}',R2)
add(39,'Blank arguments and a module variant remain an outline','095','unproved claim','Indétermination; Autom. d’un relèvement; Variante pour Λ-modules',r'''The ambiguity and automorphism lines leave their Ext arguments blank, and the module variant is announced without a formula or proof. This **differs from** the complete expressions in Hua’s “(C) Déformations”, which fill those blanks from the preceding obstruction term. The source gives the degree pattern2,1,0 and a side Hom expression; it does not specify every functorial identification needed to make a deformation groupoid theorem.''','defg','Variante|arguments|automorphismes')
add(40,'Stable differential modules of high-level truncated BT groups','095','unproved claim','Lettre Illusie 5.3; loc libres de même rang',r'''The proposition states local freeness and equal rank of ω and n under the p^N-annihilation and level hypotheses. This **supports** Hua, findings register, 129-colie-truncated-bt-package, but the cited letter is absent and no argument is written on this sheet. The exact threshold n≥N is locally marked as doubtful in the transcript and should be checked independently before extracting a quantitative theorem.''','trunc','5.3|localement libres|n \\geq N',R2)
add(41,'Inclusion and projection stabilize opposite differential terms','095','unproved claim','induit un isom; l’hom nul',r'''The page gives four maps: inclusion stabilizes ω and, after a level gap, kills n; projection stabilizes n and kills ω. Hua **restates** this pattern, but a later proof must preserve the source’s placement of the extra inequality n′≤n−N instead of treating all four assertions as unconditional. This is the finite-level mechanism relevant to the deferred lifting argument on091, not simply a list of isomorphic modules.''','trunc,def','projection|inclusion|homomorphisme nul|n - N',R2)
add(42,'The final ω–n isomorphism is asserted without its construction','095','unproved claim',r'si $n\geq2N$, $\omega_{G(n)}\simeq n_{G(n)}$',r'''The source gives the isomorphism after the two-level bound but does not identify its connecting morphism or prove canonicity. It **restates** Hua’s truncated-BT subsection and **adds** a precise target for comparing the preceding inclusion/projection maps. One should recover the map from the relevant exact sequence rather than infer a canonical identification merely from equal local rank.''','trunc','2N|omega|isomorphisme',R2)
add(43,'The truncated Ext comparison has a published locator','096','unproved claim',r'$\underline{\mathrm{Ext}}^1_{\Lambda_n}(G,M)=0$; $\underline{\mathrm{Ext}}^2_{\Lambda_n}\simeq\underline{\mathrm{Ext}}^2_{\mathbf Z}$',r'''The theorem is stated without proof on096 and **supports** Hua’s co-Lie/truncated-BT entry. Illusie, Astérisque127, Proposition2.2.4, equations(2.2.4.1–2), pp.162–164, supplies a directly matching published locator [I85]. This checks the contextual literature lead at statement level; it is not a new proof of the theorem or a verification of every bound in095.''','trunc','Ext|2.2|annulé',R2)
add(44,'The odd-characteristic vanishing is a separate, broader assertion','096, NB','unproved claim',r'$\underline{\mathrm{Ext}}^2_{\mathbf Z}(G,M)=0$ si $2$ inv., pour $\forall G$ fini loc. libre',r'''The NB extends beyond truncated BT groups to all finite locally free G, and its underlining identifies a sheaf Ext assertion. It **supports** the wording recorded under Hua, findings register, 129-ext2-vanishing-p-odd, but the page gives no proof and does not describe the topology in this short line. The omission of p=2 signals a limitation of this vanishing shortcut, not a claim that BT deformation existence fails at2.''','trunc','2|annulation|Breen',R3)
add(45,'The “no reference found” lead has a partial primary-source match','096','unpursued implication','cela simplifie la théorie des déformations pour les BT en car. p≠2',r'''Illusie’s Remark4.6(a), p.178, explicitly cites Breen for the odd-p vanishing with additive coefficients and contrasts a characteristic2 example [I85]. Breen’s theorem and Remark3, pp.339–340, address sheaf Ext vanishing for finite flat groups against smooth quasi-projective groups, including additive coefficients [B75]. This **updates** the no-reference claim in the supplied findings entry to a source-backed match in that scope; extension to every quasi-coherent M in the exact generality of the manuscript remains to be checked.''','trunc','129-ext2|annulation|Ext',R3)
add(46,'Sheaf vanishing does not assert global Ext² vanishing','096','own idea',r'$\underline{\mathrm{Ext}}^2$',r'''A global reading would be too strong: for G=Z/p and M=O on a characteristic-p base, the constant-sheaf resolution Z→Z of Z/p gives global Ext²(G,O)≅H²(O)⊕H¹(O), since multiplication by p is zero. Thus a base with nonzero H¹(O) supplies a test that distinguishes the global statement from the underlined local one; this **adds** a safeguard to the findings entry rather than refuting the manuscript. Confidence is high for this formal calculation, and Breen’s local-to-global discussion [B75, Remark2] confirms that the two notions must be kept separate.''','trunc','Ext|faisceau|129-ext2',R3,'high')
add(47,'The Tate-object lemma is genuinely about arbitrary f','098, 100, 102','unpursued implication','Catégorie Abélienne; muni d’un endomorphisme f',r'''The manuscript explicitly starts with an arbitrary endomorphism in an abelian category and f^n=0, before any specialization to multiplication by p. This **confirms** the general-f scope of Hua, findings register, 129-tate-object-one-index, and answers the orientation’s first test. It should not be reduced to a lemma only about p-primary group schemes, although the later application does require representability and flatness.''','tate,tateg','endomorphisme|catégorie abélienne|129-tate',R1)
add(48,'The one-index implication is worked out, not merely listed','098–100, upper half of100','unpursued implication',r'$(iv)\Longrightarrow(ii\ bis)$; $S_j\Longrightarrow S_1$; OK',r'''Scan100 descends the kernel–image inclusion from S_j to S_1, then uses the filtered isomorphism A/f^(n−1)A→fA to reach the graded criterion. This **supports and sharpens** Hua’s findings entry: the difficult implication has an actual revised proof sketch, rather than only an equivalence asserted on098. The proof still uses element notation and a short filtered-graded step; those are the exact places for a categorical write-up, not grounds to call the whole direction absent.''','tate','S_j|filtration|iv|one-index',R1)
add(49,'The one-index formulation has an n=1 boundary','098','own idea',r'$n\in\mathbb N^*$; $1\leq j\leq n-1$',r'''At n=1 the existential condition(iv) has no admissible j, whereas the tautological endpoint identities can hold, so the full list cannot literally be equivalent in that case. This **qualifies** the findings entry and Hua’s proposition, whose proof itself only calls(iii)⇒(iv) trivial for n≥2. Confidence is high: the issue follows from the empty index interval, without changing the substantive one-index argument for n≥2.''','tate','n \\geq 1|n \\geq 2|one-index',R1,'high')
add(50,'The graded polynomial map must be bounded or truncated','098','own idea',r'θ est un isom en degrés … n−1',r'''The register’s shorthand “bijectivity of gr⁰[t]→gr_f” suppresses the manuscript’s degree restriction. For nonzero gr⁰(A), the untruncated polynomial object has nonzero degree-n input mapping to zero because f^n=0, so its whole map cannot be injective; the correct comparison needs degrees0 through n−1 or the corresponding truncated polynomial object. This **qualifies** the contextual summary, with high confidence from the displayed grading, and leaves the handwritten letter T/Λ unresolved.''','tate','gr|bijectivité|degrés|129-tate',R1,'high')
add(51,'The strict and weak degree bounds disagree on the sheet','098','reading question','en degrés <n−1; i≤n−1',r'''In(i bis) the prose bound is strict while its following indexed reformulation includes n−1; the first bound in(i) is also locally doubtful. Hua **regularizes** the bounds to include the top nonzero degree. The later use of θ^(n−1) explains why that is mathematically natural, but the discrepancy is part of the source and must remain a reading question.''','tate','i bis|n-1|monomorphisme',R1)
add(52,'An additive rank is introduced only after the equivalence proof','100','unproved claim',r'fonction … valeurs entières; $\operatorname{rg}(A)=n\operatorname{rg}(A/fA)$',r'''The equality follows under the added rank-function hypotheses and is not an assumption of the earlier arbitrary-abelian-category lemma. Hua **adds** positivity to present a rank inequality and an iff criterion, whereas the source here explicitly states the implication to the equality. Any converse extracted from rank comparisons should say which faithfulness and sign assumptions on the rank function are being used.''','tate','positivité|rang|valeurs entières',R1)
add(53,'The equal-degree morphism hypotheses need a local rereading','100–102','reading question','conditions analogues pour (g,n); compatible aux f,g',r'''The frozen reading on100 has (g,n), while Hua reports (g,m) and supplies equal degree as an additional hypothesis. Both accounts agree that equal residual rank and compatibility are essential to the mono/epi/isomorphism comparisons developed on102. This **differs** at the local letter reading and should be revisited on the image, without retrospectively changing the frozen n.''','tate','m = n|g, m|même degré|Morphismes',R1)
add(54,'Uniqueness of Tate subobjects and quotients depends on equal residual rank','102, 104','unproved claim','seul sous-objet; corollaire dual; kif-kif pour quotients',r'''The uniqueness assertions are restricted by degree and the specified residual Tate rank; they are not uniqueness of arbitrary subgroups of a given order. Hua’s corollaries **restate** the categorical argument, while104 transports it to finite flat group schemes and abbreviates the quotient discussion. A reconstruction should keep the kernel/image identification and the equal-rank comparison explicit, particularly when the base is disconnected.''','tate,tateg','seul|quotient|rang',R1)
add(55,'The fibrewise criterion is accompanied by a flatness argument','102, 104','unpursued implication','critère de platitude; indépendamment du choix de j',r'''The page introduces H=Ker f^j and the induced map from G, then invokes fibrewise faithful flatness to obtain flatness of H and the map. This **supports** the group-scheme part of Hua’s one-index entry and identifies where the hypotheses “plat” and “de présentation finie” do real work. Hua gives an EGA locator, but this return has not independently checked that exact EGA numbering or every scope condition of the invoked criterion.''','tateg','platitude|fibres|H =|j',R1)
add(56,'The Frobenius page is an analogue, not a literal application of an endomorphism lemma','090, 098, 102–104','unpursued implication','f-coadiques; endomorphisme f',r'''Hua explicitly links the Frobenius criteria to the later Tate objects. These pages **support** the analogy in their kernel–image and graded language, but relative Frobenius lands on a twisted group rather than automatically being an endomorphism of a fixed object. A general-base comparison must therefore build the twisting into the category or the system; it cannot simply substitute F for f in every formula.''','frob,tate,tateg','Frobenius|torsion|rapprochement',R1)
add(57,'Level one is not detected by p-nilpotence alone','098, 102–104','unpursued implication','f^n=0; groupe de Tate',r'''Hua’s group-scheme subsection explicitly warns that identifying f=p Tate objects with truncated BT groups needs the special level-one Frobenius/Verschiebung condition. The source **adds** the general endomorphism viewpoint but does not erase that distinction, andN49 shows another boundary issue at n=1 in the abstract list itself. This is a scope restriction on using the manuscript lemma, not a proposed reproof of the published truncated-BT definition.''','tateg,tate','n = 1|F =|V|tronqués',R1)
add(58,'A closure-intersection defect is isolated explicitly','106–108','unpursued implication',r'$G\prime_1\subset G\prime_2\cap G_1$; égalité ssi plat/$S$',r'''The source identifies the possible excess intersection through a kernel quotient and boxes the equality/flatness condition. Hua **reconstructs** a trait setting and interprets this as the obstruction to the desired level-two subobject. The useful addition is the obstruction’s concrete location in the exact diagram; the missing ambient hypotheses should remain visible rather than hidden by the reconstruction.''','level2','adhérence|intersection|plat|Ker')
add(59,'The α″ diagram cannot be oriented solely from its intended kernel','106','reading question','α′, α, α″',r'''The vertical strokes are drawn downward with curled lower ends and no secure arrowheads, whereas Hua orients the map whose kernel he computes upward from level1 to level2. The frozen array **differs from** that mathematical reconstruction and reproduces the strokes geometrically. A corrected proof must first distinguish inclusion maps from multiplication-by-p maps; otherwise the same label can silently designate two different morphisms.''','level2','alpha|flèches verticales|inclusions')
add(60,'The equivalence of six level-two conditions needs a generic-height hypothesis','108','unproved claim','Conditions équivalentes; hauteur 2',r'''Hua supplies the generic Tate-degree-two hypothesis and gives a Z/p subgroup of Z/p² as a counterexample without it. The source **supports** the six-condition list but does not state that hypothesis on108, so the context adds a genuine restriction rather than merely expanding an abbreviation. The correspondence between flatness, monomorphy and epimorphy should be tested under the reconstructed ambient data before the list is cited as a theorem.''','level2','générique|hypothèse|contre-exemple|degré 2')
add(61,'The scratch calculation records a questioned inductive direction','107','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified','⇒ ?; n=3',r'''The ascending calculation explicitly puts a question mark on an implication, while rotated notes explore the descending route and a small n=3 case. This **adds** the working uncertainty behind Hua’s clean kernel–image proof; it should not be taken as a retraction of the completed argument on100. Several incomplete equations and filtration labels remain local reading uncertainties rather than completed lemmas.''','level2,tate','reprend|Ker|S_1',R1)
add(62,'“Proof of 1st conj.” is a tentative Galois-dévisage proposal','107, upper pencil lines','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified','? Proof of 1st conj.',r'''The English note proposes realizing the ends of an exact sequence involving Φ(G) by G′ and G″, but neither Φ nor the referenced first conjecture is defined here. It **supports** Hua’s notice of the same gap; the heading’s last abbreviation and the hand’s attribution remain uncertain. A search for the intended conjecture should retain the Galois-dévisage language as evidence rather than identify Φ with a familiar functor by resemblance alone.''','level2,phi','1st|Galois|Phi|conjecture')
add(63,'More of the dual list is present than the modernization reports','108, middle','reading question','e*), d*), c*); b*) …; a*) …',r'''The source has a kernel-arrow condition at e*, “id un épim.” at d*, and a Tate-height-two condition on G″₂ at c*, followed by two actual ellipses. Hua’s level-two subsection compresses this list and names G″₁ in the c* condition; the frozen reading **differs** at that object and **adds** the intermediate conditions. The ellipses should remain unfinished items, not inferred dual formulas.''','level2','duale|c*)|inachevée')
add(64,'The trial example’s group orders differ from Hua’s reading','108, lower half','reading question',r'$(\mathbb Z/p^2\mathbb Z\times\mathbb Z/p^2\mathbb Z)_\eta$; $\alpha_p^{2?}$',r'''The frozen reading has two p² factors generically and a doubtful superscript2 on α_p, while Hua prints a p² factor with a p factor and no square on α_p. This **differs** materially in the proposed ranks, so his proposed example cannot be adopted without another image-level decision. The sheet supplies trial data and relations, not a complete group scheme over a specified base or a finished counterexample.''','level2','essais|alpha|fibre générique|exemples')
add(65,'The F,V,u calculations need a commutation and twisting check','108','own idea',r'$V=uF$; $V^2=up$; $F^2=u^{-1}p$',r'''Hua already flags the commutation needed to derive V²=up from the preceding relations; the drawn C→C^(p)→C adds a second issue, namely whether all products have compatible twisted sources and targets. A useful test is to rewrite these as typed semilinear maps before seeking a family with the proposed special fibre. This **extends** Hua’s diagnostic with medium confidence: the obstruction is formal, but the intended identifications of C with its twists are themselves doubtful on the sheet.''','level2','commute|V^{2}|u^{-1}|Frobenius','','medium')
add(66,'The Φ criteria are more legible and more numerous here','110','reading question','u(1):G(1)−H(1); u(n):G(n)−H(n); (iv bis) Φ(G)−Φ(H) est surjectif',r'''Conditions(i) and(ii) refer to level-one and level-n group maps, not an unreadable class of points, and(iv bis) is filled with Φ-surjectivity. This **contradicts** Hua’s description of those locations as partly pointwise/unreadable and empty, while leaving the base abbreviation “sur le T.K.” unresolved. The six conditions can now be studied individually; their equivalence is still unproved and cannot be asserted without determining the base and Φ.''','phi','iv bis|illisibles|points|conditions')
add(67,'Exactness alone is explicitly denied as a smoothness argument','110; compare088','unproved claim','not⇒ ker est [formellt] lisse',r'''The negative implication and circled insertion explicitly reject a formal-smoothness conclusion for the kernel from a short exact sequence alone. This **supports** Hua’s opening of sectionVIII and complements the cancelled kernel-is-BT assertion on088 without identifying the two claims. The later equivalence list and conservativity statement require additional hypotheses; the source does not derive them from the rejected implication.''','phi,extensions','noyau|lisse|suite exacte')
add(68,'The unknown functor should be tested for lattice sensitivity','107, 110','own idea','Φ est “conservatif”; Φ(u) pas surjectif',r'''The Galois-dévisage language, finite-level conditions and failure of surjectivity for certain formally flat maps suggest testing candidate Φ functors on integral lattices rather than only on rationalized generic fibres. This **adds** a strategy to Hua’s refusal to identify Φ, not an identification of the functor. Confidence is low because the base abbreviation and the actual definition are missing; a candidate must satisfy every condition on110, including the now-readable(iv bis).''','phi,level2','Phi|conservatif|Galois','','low')
add(69,'The reused sheets form two independent partial texts','099, 101, 103, 105, 109, 111','unpursued implication','n°380; n°382',r'''Printed pages47,48,51 of n°380 concern function algebras, while15,18,19 of n°382 concern convergent series and analyticity. This **adds** content that Hua’s “Les stations” explicitly omits and reconstructs the partial reading orders103→099→101 and111→109→105, with intervening printed pages missing. Their physical adjacency to Tate-object calculations is not evidence that the mathematical arguments are connected or that the reused typescripts are authored by Grothendieck.''','stations','duplicateur|380|382|étranger')
add(70,'The function-algebra representation rests on missing earlier propositions','103','unproved claim','transformation de Gelfand; Prop.10, Cor.',r'''The reused sheet states the isometric Gelfand representation of bounded continuous functions and the pullback/evaluation constructions, citing an earlier proposition and leaving a corollary number blank. This **adds** a substantial unrelated text to the supplied context, whose modernized reading does not reproduce it. The claim’s dependencies belong to that typescript’s preceding functional-analysis argument, not to the BT lemmas on neighbouring scans.''','stations','Gelfand|Algèbres de fonctions|étranger')
add(71,'The Stone–Čech page has a missing adjective, not a completed topology phrase','099, continuing103','reading question','la topologie la [blank] fine; compactifié modèle Stone Cech',r'''The blank before “fine” and the literal “modèle Stone Cech” should be checked against the source text if that reused typescript is later identified. This **adds** a reading problem absent from Hua’s scope, while the surrounding proposition and density argument clearly locate the intended compactification construction. Neither the expected adjective nor a standard spelling should replace what the scan actually supplies.''','stations','Stone|compactifié|étranger')
add(72,'Closed graph and “closed equivalence relation” are explicitly distinguished','101','unpursued implication','le graphe de T(A) est fermé; n’est pas nécessairement fermée',r'''The two consecutive sentences use different notions of closedness, followed by compact-saturation and T(T′(R))=R assertions under stated hypotheses. This **adds** a terminological issue not covered by Hua’s omitted reused sheets. A comparison with modern quotient-space language must first recover the typescript’s definition of a “relation fermée”, rather than treat the two sentences as an immediate contradiction.''','stations','relation|fermé|étranger')
add(73,'The compact-saturation proof compresses a finite-intersection argument','101','own idea','Puisque K est compact, l’intersection de ces voisinages … rencontre K',r'''The written proof needs finite intersections of the closed saturated neighbourhoods to meet K, not merely each one separately. A direct completion is to intersect finitely many corresponding open saturated neighbourhoods around t, use that t is adherent to the saturation of K, and then pass to a point of K in the same equivalence class. This **adds** a proof detail absent from the contextual treatment, with high confidence because saturation and the closure assumption supply exactly the finite-intersection property needed by compactness.''','stations','compact|relation|étranger','','high')
add(74,'The divided-derivative composition law conflicts with the normalization','109; compare105','own idea',r'$\Delta^s\circ\Delta^t=\Delta^{s+t}$; $D^s=s!\Delta^s$',r'''For one variable over R, applying the two formulas to x² with s=t=1 gives2 on the left and1 on the right, so the displayed composition law and the normalization cannot both hold literally. The expected binomial coefficient is a diagnostic from this calculation, not an emendation of the frozen typescript; it also explains why the bound on105 need not display a factorial. This **adds** an independently checkable source inconsistency outside Hua’s scope, with high confidence from the explicit polynomial test.''','stations','Delta|analytic|étranger','','high')
add(75,'Analyticity criteria are stated without their missing proof context','105, 109','unproved claim','Critères d’analyticité; principe du prolongement analytique',r'''The reused sheets state derivative bounds characterizing real analyticity, a complex differentiability criterion, Taylor expansion and analytic continuation/contact assertions. This **adds** material absent from Hua’s reading, but their surrounding numbered definitions and proofs are not all present. The D^s versus Δ^s normalization must be kept separate from the actual missing binomial coefficient inN74.''','stations','analytic|382|étranger')
add(76,'Convergence geometry belongs to the analytic typescript','111','unproved claim','l’indicatrice de convergence; logarithmiquement convexe',r'''The sheet asserts logarithmic convexity, descriptions of strict convergence domains and finite-dimensional tensor compatibility, with no proofs on the supplied page. This **adds** a second cluster of analytic claims to the contextual inventory and explains the role of the double-brace versus double-bracket series spaces in the transcription. No inference from this occurrence of convergence to the p-adic crystalline programme is justified by physical reuse alone.''','stations','convergence|382|étranger')
add(77,'The Serre–Tate equivalence is announced with incomplete hypotheses','113','unproved claim','Th. de Serre–Tate; une équivalence de catégories',r'''The triple involving A_o, p-power torsion and φ is visible, but the small text under S_o⊂S is weak and the theorem is not proved. Hua **adds** nilpotence assumptions and the meaning of φ in “1) et2)”; those are reconstructed mathematical data, not all legible words on this page. This is relevant background to the A.57 lead, but not a global existence theorem for correspondences.''','nice12','Serre|nilpotent|triplets','ANABELIAN_SUCCESSOR A.57 [Open, return’s claim; indirect connection only]')
add(78,'The Nice definition reuses the earlier height convention with a different letter','088, 113','unpursued implication','Si G(1) de rang p^d, alors G(n) de rang p^{nd}',r'''Here d measures BT height through the rank of G(1), whereas the margin on090 uses d in the dimension-duality relation. This **supports** Hua’s explicit convention warning and shows why a global replacement of d by “dimension” would be wrong. The three defining conditions and the rank-growth claim are an outline, not a new proof of the torsion representability established elsewhere.''','nice12,extensions,frob','hauteur|dimension|p^{nh}')
add(79,'The divided-power addition formula repeats x','113','reading question',r'$(x+y)^{(n)}=\sum_{i+j=n}x^{(i)}x^{(j)}$',r'''The frozen formula has x in both factors, while Hua prints the usual x-and-y expression. The source thus **differs from** the modernized formula, independently of his change from parenthesized to bracketed divided powers. Any mathematical explanation may discuss the usual identity, but the repeated variable is a literal source anomaly, not a reason to alter the transcription.''','nice3','x+y|puissances|identités')
add(80,'Nilpotence and the weaker logarithm condition are distinguished','113','unproved claim','quasi nilpotentes; (n−1)!J^{(n)}=0',r'''The page assigns the exponential to the nilpotent divided-power case and the logarithm to the factorial-torsion case it calls quasi-nilpotent. Hua **restates** the distinction and warns that this terminology is not simply the modern connection-theoretic notion. The displayed formulas motivate a precise comparison of the two filtrations; they do not by themselves construct the crystalline site or prove that all relevant infinite sums are defined.''','nice3','quasi-nilpotentes|logarithme|exponentielle')
add(81,'The crystalline-site heading is revised on the sheet','113','withdrawn in the same text','Site cristallin absolu [deleted]; (absolu) [inserted]',r'''The first heading is struck through, and the later heading replaces a doubtful “naturel/relatif” word by “(absolu)”. This **differs from** Hua’s neutral “absolu, ou relatif” exposition by preserving the direction of revision. The clipped lower line and canonical divided powers of p still leave the exact site convention incomplete in the outline.''','nice3','absolu|relatif|site cristallin')
add(82,'Crystals over a perfect ring are described by Witt modules','114','unproved claim','les cristaux sur A_o sont les Modules loc. libres sur A',r'''The example asserts the perfect-ring comparison without describing evaluation, the pro-PD thickening or completeness conventions. It **restates** Hua’s first crystalline example, which is a conceptual guide rather than a proof of the assertion for every perfect ring. Those conventions become necessary when interpreting D(G_o)_A on115.''','nice3,nice4','parfait|W(A|modules localement libres')
add(83,'A Frobenius lift is deleted; quasi-nilpotence is not written','114','withdrawn in the same text','[et F:X−X remontant Frobenius …] [deleted]',r'''The example retains a smooth formal lift and a flat relative connection after cancelling the Frobenius-lift clause. Hua **supports** that cancellation but **adds** topological quasi-nilpotence to obtain his crystalline interpretation. The deletion and the missing condition should be catalogued separately: cancelling a chosen Frobenius lift does not itself establish the full connection/crystal equivalence.''','nice3','connexion|quasi-nilpotente|barre')
add(84,'Full faithfulness is explicitly conjectural in the integral crystalline programme','114','conjecture','Il se pourrait que le foncteur … soit pl. fidèle',r'''The phrasing marks full faithfulness over the indicated characteristic-p base as an expectation. It **restates** Hua’s programmeII, but his historical comments and literature-from-memory do not settle the assertion in this generality. A research statement must specify the base class and the category of Dieudonné crystals before testing the claim.''','nice4','pleine fidélité|Il se pourrait|hypothèses')
add(85,'The essential image is expected but never characterized','114','conjecture','j’ai une idée de ce qui pourrait être l’image essentielle',r'''The sentence gives neither a criterion nor a list of objects in that image. It **supports** Hua’s explicit limit on what the folder establishes, and should remain a distinct open item from full faithfulness. The formal and filtered descriptions on115 suggest possible constraints but do not provide a general essential-image theorem on the preceding base.''','nice4','image essentielle|idée|programme')
add(86,'The Dieudonné crystal’s construction and base-change compatibility are announced','114','unproved claim','On définit “foncteur de Dieudonné”; compatible aux chgt. de base',r'''The outline asserts the contravariant functor, its locally free crystal values and the F,V relations in characteristic p without constructing the crystal or the operators. This **restates** Hua’s programmeI–II and links back to the classical dictionary on082. Distinguishing construction, compatibility and full faithfulness prevents the explicit conjecture ofN84 from being mistaken for the status of every preceding line.''','nice4,dieu','changement de base|foncteur|F_M')
add(87,'Classical compatibility is a separate required comparison','082, 115','unproved claim','on retrouve le foncteur de Dieudonné habituel',r'''ProgrammeIII asserts recovery of the perfect-field functor, rather than proving it by the preceding F,V identities alone. It **restates** Hua’s classical-to-crystalline comparison. A proof must identify the variance, twist and normalization of the classical module, including the distinctions that the draft on082 does not spell out consistently.''','nice4,dieu','habituel|classique|parfait')
add(88,'The first Hodge term remains an overwritten reading','115','reading question',r'$\omega_{G^*}$ with a deleted overmark',r'''The frozen reading preserves a doubtful first ω_(G*) and its cancellation, while Hua reconstructs ω_G so that the final quotient is t_(G*). This **differs from** his completed Hodge sequence and bears directly on the proposed comparison with Δ on094. The expected sequence is a strong mathematical prompt for further image inspection, not a replacement for the visible revision.''','nice4,colie','premier|omega_G|surchargé|Hodge',R2)
add(89,'The fibre restriction contains a negation absent from Hua’s paraphrase','115','reading question','les fibres n’aient pas de comp. toroïdale; ou … soient connexes',r'''The frozen wording negates the toroidal-component condition, whereas Hua says the page restricts to toroidal or connected fibres. This **differs substantively** from his paraphrase; the connectors around the overwritten divided-power hypothesis remain doubtful, so the exact disjunction should not yet be formalized. The later deformation-filtration equivalence is asserted, but this page alone does not decide whether any fibre condition is redundant.''','nice4','fibres|toroïdales|nilpotentes')
add(90,'Full faithfulness up to isogeny is asserted, not phrased like the preceding conjecture','115','unproved claim','est pleinement fidèle',r'''ProgrammeV uses an affirmative sentence for the functor to special-fibre data with a filtration, unlike the “Il se pourrait” on114. Hua **weakens** it to a conditional “serait” and adds a Noetherian assumption in the first sentence, while the manuscript’s later local example carries that qualifier. The target category is left as an unreadable/unfinished label, so the claimed fully faithful functor still lacks a complete definition and proof here.''','nice4','isogénie|serait|noethérien|pleinement')
add(91,'The last example broadens a deleted discrete-valuation hypothesis','115, bottom','withdrawn in the same text','de valuation discrète [deleted]; local complet noeth, à corps résiduel [inserted]',r'''The visible revision replaces the discrete-valuation wording by a complete local base with a doubtful “noeth” qualifier and perfect residue field. This **differs from** Hua’s final example, which returns to a complete DVR and can therefore understate the manuscript’s claimed scope. The broadened statement is still unproved and the tensor construction requires its base maps to be specified; no classification theorem for that whole class of rings is established by the deletion alone.''','nice4','valuation|noeth|local complet')
add(92,'Rationalization and the map from W(k) need to be specified','115, last line','reading question',r'$(M\otimes_W A)_{p|\mathbb Q}$',r'''The final subscript looks p-like and is marked doubtful, whereas Hua interprets it as inverting p. This **supports** a rationalization reading as mathematical context, but the incomplete target category and unspecified W(k)-algebra structure remain independent gaps. In particular the revised general local-base assertion ofN91 cannot be justified solely by importing the interpretation appropriate to a chosen p-adic coefficient ring.''','nice4','rendu|rationnel|M \\otimes_W|1/p')
add(93,'The cancellation pattern separates definitions from choices of construction','080–081, 088, 095, 114–115','own idea','antiéquivalence; Ext_Z; Frobenius clause deleted',r'''A useful editorial study would classify revisions by their mathematical effect: corrected variance, rejected closure properties, coefficient-category changes, and removal or broadening of auxiliary hypotheses. This **adds** a cross-page comparison to Hua’s list of departures, while avoiding an unsupported chronological ordering of the loose leaves. Confidence is medium: the cancellations are visible, but their dates and some attachment points are not.''','stations,witt,extensions,defg,nice3,nice4','Substantive|barré|corrig|hypothèse','','medium')
add(94,'A divided-power bridge to the de Rham programme remains only a lead','113–115','own idea','Puissances divisées; (n−1)!J^{(n)}=0',r'''WG2 §2.4 #5 proposes relating divided powers in the 1976 de Rham notes to integrality questions in other cotes; the explicit factorial-torsion condition here offers a concrete comparison datum. This **adds** a possible bridge from the BT/crystalline side, but neither those other manuscripts nor their full arguments are supplied, and no equality of the two constructions is established. Confidence is low until one compares the actual cochain operations and filtrations, rather than the shared phrase “divided powers”.''','nice3,nice4','WG2|divided powers|puissances|intégralité','WG2 §2.4 #5 [unchecked planning question; the other cotes are not supplied]','low')
add(95,'The folder date does not date every deformation leaf','091, 095, 113–115','reading question','lettre Illusie, 6.4; Lettre Illusie 5.3; Conférence à Nice',r'''No secure calendar date is written on these selected leaves, and the letter numbers should not be converted into dates. Hua likewise treats the folder dating and the Nice-address identification as contextual rather than page-internal evidence. Illusie’s introduction associates his reconstruction of the deformation proof with Grothendieck’s June1971 course [I85, p.151], which **adds** a chronology question but does not itself redetermine the date of091 or095.''','stations,def,trunc,nice4','datation|1970|Illusie|Nice',R2)
add(96,'The contextual scan concordance includes a reused page','098, 100, 102–104','reading question','scan103: §4. Algèbres de fonctions; n°380',r'''The findings-row locator for 129-tate-object-one-index includes scan103, which is a reused function-algebra typescript, while the group-scheme argument actually runs across102 and104. Hua’s page102 corresponds to this reused scan103, so the subsection and title anchors resolve the offset more reliably than the ambiguous prose sentence about numbering in RESULTS_STATE. This **corrects** a locator in the context without changing the substantive one-index finding.''','stations,tate,tateg','129-tate|pages 98|copies|101–103',R1)
add(97,'The Tate-object cover title remains a reading question','097, first word','reading question','Sotties | Sottises',r'''The frozen title’s first word is low-confidence, whereas Hua consistently calls this section “Sorites”. The mathematical sequence of short propositions makes his reading plausible, but that contextual fit does not decide the letters on the cover. This **differs from** the modernized title and records its alternative after freezing, without changing either the page file or the original doubt record.''','stations,tate','Sorites|chemise|96')
add(98,'The divided-power ideal-membership condition needs an index restriction','113, upper-right formula','reading question',r'$x^{(0)}=1$; $x^{(n)}\in J$',r'''The source writes both the zero-th divided power and ideal membership without an explicit n≥1 qualifier next to the latter. Hua **supplies** that qualifier, which matters for a proper ideal because its absence can be read as forcing1 into J. This is an implicit-condition question in the lecture outline, distinct from the repeated variable in the addition formula (N79).''','nice3','x^{[0]}|n \\geq 1|puissances divisées')
